In article <10493@pasteur.Berkeley.EDU> benji@euler.Berkeley.EDU (Benji Rudiak-Gould) writes:
>This may be just another confusion of terminology, but shouldn't a ring of
>regeneration act like an amulet of life saving?

Perhaps...  Technically, with a ring of regeneration (as listed in the AD&D
Dungeon Masters Guide) the only way to prevent it from bringing a person
back to life is in the case of "total destruction of all living tissue by fire or acid or similar means."  (This is why eating a troll kills him permanently -
stomach acid.)  Also, the ring must stay on the corpse (at least a bit of it)
for it to work, so if a monster or another adventurer comes along and picks
up the ring, your chances of regenerating are nil.  

I suppose the dev team just decided that it would be a pain to program it so
that after say 3,000 turns you came back to life, so they eliminated that
aspect, assuming that in nethack's busy dungeon, somebody would come along
and take the ring long before it would have a chance to bring you back to life.

